Chemical spills exposure
Social & Environmental
History of acute proximity, Chemical burns/mucous membrane irritation, Dyspnea/ARDS, Positive toxicological screening

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: SEV-035
Reason for Visit: Acute severe respiratory distress and chemical burns following an accidental hazardous materials release.
Location: Private residence (adjacent to an agricultural supply depot)
Present at Visit: Patient, partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient who had just fled an accidental chemical vapor cloud, now experiencing severe choking, shortness of breath, and burning eyes. Urgent assessment was required to evaluate for acute toxic inhalation, provide immediate medical stabilization, and prevent airway compromise prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "A yellow cloud blew over the fence, and now my chest is burning, I can't catch my breath, and my eyes are stinging."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 30-minute history of sudden onset of dyspnea, choking, and severe irritation of the eyes and mucous membranes. Key symptoms stem directly from a history of acute proximity to an industrial accident (an agricultural chemical spill/vapor release next door). The patient is coughing persistently, producing frothy sputum, indicating acute airway irritation and impending pulmonary edema. They report a severe headache, dizziness, and a burning sensation on the exposed skin of their forearms. The patient exhibits extreme anxiety and a fear of impending suffocation.
Functional Status: Independent but acutely incapacitated by severe respiratory distress and chemical irritation
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate threat to life from airway swelling/ARDS, ongoing environmental contamination, need for local evacuation

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 160/95 | HR: 125 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 30 | SpO2: 88% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Agitated, coughing continuously, severe conjunctival injection, tearing profusely, smelling of a sharp, acrid chemical odor
Physical Exam: Respiratory exam reveals widespread expiratory wheeze and harsh, transmitted upper airway sounds (stridor developing). Auscultation demonstrates fine basal crackles, suggesting early alveolar fluid exudate (chemical pneumonitis). Skin exam shows diffuse erythematous rashes (chemical irritation) on the bilateral forearms. Cardiovascular exam shows significant tachycardia. Diagnostic tetrad features are starkly present: history of acute proximity to a spill, mucous membrane irritation, dyspnea pointing to ARDS, and a high likelihood of positive toxicological screening.
Environmental Observations: Faint acrid smell is noticeable even inside the house; partner is also coughing mildly.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Chemical spills exposure (Acute Chemical Pneumonitis / Toxic Inhalation) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials: 1. Acute Asthma Exacerbation (Allergic) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The profound conjunctival burns, erythematous skin rash, and the explicit, undeniable history of sudden exposure to a visible chemical vapor cloud.
2. Carbon Monoxide Poisoning - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of severe, painful mucous membrane irritation and choking (CO is an odorless, non-irritating asphyxiant gas).
3. Panic Attack - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The objective, life-threatening derangements in vital signs including significant hypoxia (SpO2 88%), tachycardia, and the physical presence of frothy sputum and wheeze.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is in an acute, life-threatening respiratory crisis due to direct toxic chemical inhalation, presenting with severe hypoxia, bronchospasm, and signs of impending acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS). Delaying this assessment would have resulted in rapid airway occlusion, massive pulmonary edema, and death. The objective findings of hypoxia, tachypnea, and chemical burns completely justify the urgent after-hours MBS intervention to rapidly initiate high-flow oxygen, aggressive bronchodilator therapy, and coordinate an immediate emergency extraction and HAZMAT response.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate removal of the patient's contaminated outer clothing. Immediate administration of high-flow oxygen via a non-rebreather mask. Continuous nebulized salbutamol. Copious irrigation of the eyes with normal saline. Urgent notification of 000 for intensive care ambulance and fire department HAZMAT unit.
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking off your contaminated shirt right now and leaving it outside, you instantly stop breathing in the chemical fumes, which halts the burning in your lungs and allows the oxygen mask to work effectively.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Activation of state environmental protection and public health emergency response.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to the Emergency Department resuscitation team and toxicologist.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate priority transport to the nearest Emergency Department. The patient must not be allowed to return to the contaminated area. Close monitoring of the airway is critical as chemical swelling can occur rapidly.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The chemical has burned the lining of your lungs. The hospital must monitor you continuously because this type of injury can cause sudden fluid build-up in the lungs hours after the exposure.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Keep the oxygen mask securely on your face. Do not rub your eyes. The ambulance is arriving to take you to the hospital for specialized monitoring.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The chemical vapor has caused massive inflammation in your airways, making them swell shut. The oxygen and the nebulizer are forcing them open. Removing your clothes stopped further exposure.
Actionable Steps: Breathe slowly and deeply through the mask. Let the paramedics handle everything. Inform the hospital staff exactly what the neighboring business stores so they can consult the poisons center.

